  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 \n\nTropical moisture from the remnants of Isaias enters the region\ntoday. Widespread rainfall pushes into NEPA this morning and\nwill spread northward across the area throughout the day. Rain\nwill be steady through day and be moderate to heavy at times. \nNEPA into the Southern Tier has potential to see localized \nrainfall amounts of 1 to 2 inches with less than inch expected \nacross most of Central NY and the Finger Lakes Region. Although \nsome minor urban flooding issues could occur, especially in poor\ndrainage areas, the flash flood threat will be very low due to \nthe dry antecedent conditions in place. \n\nRain will exit the region from south to north late tonight\nthrough early Monday morning. Warm and drier conditions will \nreturn on Monday, although skies will be mostly cloudy across \nmuch of the area and drizzle may linger into Monday morning. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2\n\nA surface low is forecast to track across southern Ontario,\nCanada through the day on Tuesday. A shortwave impulse and\nassociated surface cold will push through the region Tuesday\nevening. During the day, our CWA will initially be well within \nthe warm sector of this system, with southwest winds and warm \ntemperatures expected. This should allow surface instability to \nincrease ahead of the frontal passage if the timing works out. \nModel soundings show CAPE values ranging up to 500-1000 J/Kg\nwest of the I81 corridor, along with 30-40 knots of 0-6km bulk \nshear. PWATs also increase to around 1.4 in, so any convective \nshowers that do form will also have the potential to produce \nheavy downpours. Forecast high temperatures have increased some\nfrom the previous forecast, with temperatures in the mid to\nupper 70s possible west of I81 and highs in the mid-60s to low \n70s elsewhere.\n\nBehind this frontal system the latest guidance keeps it mainly dry \nand seasonably cool for Wednesday and Thursday next week. The next \nchance for rain will be Friday, into Saturday as a potentially deep \ntrough approaches the forecast area. This could bring much cooler \ntemperatures for next weekend and possibly the first snowflakes\nof the season, stay tuned.",
